e if the slower  burn of everybody's gone to the rapture  leaves you skeptical then let's pivot to  another game this time focusing on a  crew stuck together in the confines of  an interstellar installation  Homa the inhabitants of the spacestation  are long gone but the principle game  mechanic enables you to load up  conversations throughout the station and  watch holographic representations of the  characters interact these scenes can be  replayed fast forwarded investigated and  explored like a moving narrative diorama  again you don't interact with the  characters but you can watch their  relationships evolve and see how they  face issues solve problems and alter  allegiances in the face of disaster  using your own initiative you can  explore scenes as deeply as you want  which lets you witness a large handful  of downbeat and casual moments between  the characters which work to better  inform who they are and increase your  ability to empathize with them and their  plight this is taken to the next level  in another game which takes you out of  the shoes of a disaster investigator and  into those of an insurance auditor the  return of the Overton  Oh burden plays out like a massive game  of Master Mind you know that board game  with the pegs when examining a corpse  aboard the ER burden you use a creepy  pocket watch to view the final moments  of the crewmembers life each scene  begins with a brief audio only context  before revealing a tableau of the  unfortunate souls last moment the  tableaus are just that sprawling  unmoving dioramas for you to walk in and  around in an effort to decipher who was  who what is happening at that moment how  they died  and ultimately what happened on the ship  the game's difficulty requires you to  pay close attention to unravel the  mystery and its unique art style and  twisty turny plot make you want to while  the quality of its story is divisive I  always really enjoyed the way the  division handled the post-apocalypse and 